    for(const eye of [...scene.children].filter(o=>o.userData.catEye))cat.add(eye);

    // Per-book librarian commentary for "Ask the librarian about this book"
    const librarianNotes={11:'A girl follows a waistcoat down a hole and never quite comes back the same size twice. Every rule in it is broken with perfect courtesy. I keep it near the front desk for visitors who insist this library makes no sense.',12:'A girl steps through a mirror into a world run entirely on the logic of chess, where you must run as fast as you can simply to remain in the same place. Carroll built the sequel around a stricter, stranger set of rules than the first book’s looser dream logic. The looking-glass insects alone are worth the visit.',16:'A boy refuses, on principle, to grow up, and takes a nursery’s worth of children with him to a place built entirely around that refusal. Every adult in it is eventually revealed as a child in disguise, one way or another. Barrie understood that the fear of growing old is really the fear of being forgotten, which is not quite the same thing.',23:'A man born enslaved teaches himself to read against direct and considerable danger, correctly identifying literacy as the single thing his condition most depended on denying him. He wrote this account himself, naming names, at real personal risk. I do not have a wry line for this one; some books are simply owed a plain reading.',26:'An angel falls, builds a case for his own rebellion with genuinely persuasive rhetoric, and somehow ends up the most compelling character in a poem explicitly written to justify the ways of God to men. Milton dictated the whole epic blind, to whichever family member happened to be nearby to write it down. Whether he intended the devil to be this charismatic remains one of literature’s oldest arguments.',32:'Three men stumble into an isolated country populated entirely by women, who have built a stable, thriving civilisation with no men in it at all, and none of the disasters the explorers confidently predicted. Gilman used the premise mostly to interrogate, patiently and pointedly, every assumption the three visitors brought with them. It is utopian fiction with a very sharp, very polite edge.',33:'A woman wears a badge of shame sewn by the very people most implicated in her disgrace, and outlasts every one of their reputations by simple, stubborn dignity. The letter never quite means what the town insists it means. Hawthorne understood that public punishment mostly punishes the audience’s own conscience.',35:'A man builds a machine to visit the future and finds humanity has politely split itself into predator and prey. He goes further still, to the very end of the sun. Wells wrote it as a young man already worried about which kind of person he was becoming.',36:'Martians land in England with weapons no army of the era can answer, and the whole apparatus of empire discovers, briefly and instructively, what it feels like to be on the losing end of one. Wells wrote it as a pointed rebuke to a country very sure of its own permanence. The ending humbles the invaders rather than the defenders, and I suspect that was deliberate too.',41:'A schoolmaster falls for a farmer’s daughter and loses her to a headless horseman who may simply be a rival in a clever costume. Irving never quite confirms it either way, which is the correct choice and I will not hear an argument about it. Read it once around Halloween, the way it was clearly intended.',43:'A respectable doctor brews a formula to separate his better nature from his worse one, and discovers the worse one enjoys being let out. The twist is common knowledge now, which was not always true, and the book survives being spoiled remarkably well. Read it as a essay on restraint rather than a mystery, and it improves.',45:'An orphan arrives expecting to be useful on a farm and is instead, through sheer persistent imagination, nearly impossible to contain. She talks constantly, apologises rarely, and wins over an entire village by simply refusing to be quiet about her own inner life. I have met several visitors to this library who claim her as a personal influence, unprompted.',46:'A miserly man is shown three ghosts and, unusually for this genre, actually listens to them. It invented a good portion of what people now think Christmas is supposed to feel like. Even Quill sits differently near this one in December.',54:'A boy is transformed into a girl by enchantment and discovers an entire land already ruled by an army of opinionated, independent-minded women. Baum kept expanding Oz well past its first success, and this sequel is where he let the whole geography get considerably stranger. Ozma’s arrival here changes the entire shape of everything that follows in the series.',55:'A girl is blown to a strange country and told the answer to her problem was available the whole time. Three companions join her, each certain they already lack the thing they are about to prove they have. I keep the ruby-slippered edition slightly separate from the green one; readers seem to prefer choosing.',61:'A short, furious pamphlet arguing that history is the story of who owns what, and that the current arrangement is neither natural nor permanent. It was meant to be read aloud at meetings, not studied quietly at a desk, and you can feel that urgency in every sentence. Few documents this brief have rearranged this much of the world since.',62:'A Confederate officer is transported, without much narrative ceremony, to Mars, where he discovers lower gravity has made him unexpectedly formidable in a fight. Burroughs wrote pure escapism and never pretended otherwise. Take it for exactly what it offers and it delivers generously.',72:'A princess of a dying Martian city is fought over across red deserts and forgotten temples, in prose that never once doubts its own adventure. Burroughs wrote quickly and it shows, cheerfully, in every chapter. It is not trying to be Homer. It is trying to be fun, and mostly is.',73:'A young soldier flees his first battle, is haunted by the cowardice of it, and spends the rest of the book trying to earn back a version of himself he can live with. Crane had never seen a battlefield when he wrote it, and veterans of the actual war swore he must have. Courage, this book argues, is mostly a story you tell yourself afterward and get to revise.',74:'A boy fakes his own death mainly to enjoy attending his own funeral. Every scheme in it is committed with total sincerity and no sense of consequence whatsoever. I have never met a reader who did not, somewhere around the whitewashed fence, forgive him for all of it.',76:'A boy and an escaped man raft down a river together, and the boy spends the whole journey unlearning what his town taught him to believe was right. Twain called it the best book he ever wrote, and then argued with people for the rest of his life about what it actually meant. Skip the ending if you want to stay angry at it; I never quite manage to.',77:'A family’s fortune and misfortune both trace back to a curse laid on the house generations earlier by a man his ancestor had wronged. Hawthorne believed inherited guilt does not politely wait for the guilty party to actually be present to collect. The house itself is easily as important a character as anyone living inside it.',82:'A disinherited knight returns from crusade in disguise to find his country’s Norman and Saxon halves still refusing to speak to each other. Scott more or less invented the modern costume drama with this one. The tournament scenes alone are why half the era’s illustrators kept their jobs.',84:'A young man builds a life from spare parts and then refuses to raise it. Everyone remembers the monster; almost no one remembers he is never given a name. That omission is the whole tragedy, if you ask me.',86:'A nineteenth-century man wakes up in Camelot and immediately starts trying to industrialise it, with results that are funnier and considerably darker than the premise suggests. Twain meant it as satire of romantic nostalgia for a past that was never actually pleasant to live in. It ends far bleaker than the joke that opened it, on purpose.',90:'The son of the jungle-raised lord grows up in London civilisation and discovers, with some relief, that the jungle was rather more honest. Burroughs kept mining that same vein of ancestry and instinct book after book, and readers kept buying it. Do not read it for subtlety; read it for momentum.',98:'Two cities, one revolution, and a man who finally does something worth remembering with a life he considered wasted. The opening line has outlived nearly everything else written that decade. Keep a handkerchief nearby for the last page.',103:'A meticulously punctual Englishman wagers his entire fortune that he can circle the globe in exactly eighty days, and then does, mostly through excellent train schedules and one loyal servant’s improvisation. Verne wrote an adventure novel that is secretly also a love letter to the timetable. It remains the most cheerful bet in this catalogue.',105:'A woman refuses a proposal on other people’s advice and spends the rest of the book, and several years, quietly regretting it. It is Austen’s last finished novel and her gentlest, written by someone who had stopped needing to prove anything. Second chances, it argues, are rarer than first impressions but not impossible.',107:'A woman inherits a farm and three suitors along with it, and spends the book discovering that competence and good judgment are not the same skill. Hardy is gentler here than in his later, angrier novels, though the ache is already visible underneath. The sheep alone cause more plot than most human characters manage elsewhere on this shelf.',110:'A young woman’s one youthful mistake follows her through every subsequent chance at happiness, judged far more harshly by the society around her than by the book itself. Hardy subtitled it a pure woman, deliberately provoking readers who disagreed. It cost him a great deal of his remaining goodwill with the critics of his day, and he clearly considered that a fair trade.',113:'An unloved, unpleasant child is sent to a cold estate and finds a walled garden nobody has tended in a decade. Tending it turns out to tend her as well, and a bedridden cousin along with her. It is the rare book in which a garden is the actual protagonist and everyone concedes the point gracefully.',120:'A boy finds a map, a ship, and a one-legged cook with opinions about all of it. Long John Silver is meant to be the villain and none of us ever quite believe it. Stevenson invented half of what pirates are supposed to sound like, on this desk, more or less.',125:'A girl trades moths for an education, collecting specimens from an Indiana swamp to pay her own way toward the life she actually wants. Stratton-Porter clearly loved that swamp more than most of her human characters, and it shows in every description of it. Ambition, in this one, smells faintly of cedar and wet leaves.',132:'A concise military treatise arguing that the very best victories are the ones achieved without a battle ever needing to be fought at all. Sun Tzu wrote for generals and it has since been read by nearly everyone with any ambition to win anything. I find its actual message considerably more patient than the confrontational way it usually gets quoted.',135:'A man serves nineteen years for stealing bread, is shown one act of mercy he does not feel he deserves, and spends the rest of his life trying to earn it retroactively. An entire revolution happens mostly at the edges of his personal redemption. Hugo believed one good act, properly received, could restructure a soul; the book is his argument for that belief at enormous, patient length.',140:'A young immigrant comes to Chicago for a decent life and instead documents, meal by meal, exactly what goes into the meat his readers have been eating. Sinclair meant to write about labour and instead accidentally rewrote the country’s food safety laws. He said afterward he aimed for the public’s heart and hit its stomach.',141:'A poor relation is sent to live among wealthier cousins and becomes the only one among them with any actual principles. Austen’s least forgiving heroine to the people around her, and I mean that as praise. The house, in this one, judges its owners rather more harshly than its guest.',145:'A whole English town, watched closely enough that every compromised marriage and thwarted ambition in it feels like your own. Nobody gets the grand life they imagined at nineteen. It is the kindest possible way I know to tell you that is not the tragedy you think it is.',146:'A wealthy girl is orphaned and reduced to servitude in the very boarding school that once fawned over her fortune, and refuses, throughout, to let poverty diminish her manners or her imagination. Burnett believed dignity was a choice available regardless of circumstance, and builds the whole plot to prove it. Her attic room somehow becomes warmer than everyone else’s parlour.',147:'A blunt, furious pamphlet arguing that a colony’s loyalty to a distant king made no more sense than any other accident of birth, published anonymously and read by nearly everyone regardless. Paine wrote plainly, on purpose, for readers who had no patience for finer rhetoric. Few pamphlets in history have moved this many people to action this quickly.',151:'A sailor shoots an albatross for no particular reason and is made, by the ship and the sea and possibly the universe itself, to understand exactly what that carelessness cost. He tells the story now to strangers at weddings, compulsively, because telling it is the only penance available to him. Coleridge wrote the guilt more vividly than the crime, which is rather the correct proportion.',153:'A stonemason wants an education the world has decided is not for men of his class, and every door closes on him a little more decisively than the last. Hardy called it his bitterest book, and readers of the time agreed loudly enough that he stopped writing novels altogether afterward. I keep it in the contested section because it made people that angry, on purpose.',158:'A young woman with entirely too much time and confidence appoints herself matchmaker to her whole village, and is wrong about nearly everyone including herself. Austen called her a heroine no one but herself would much like, and then made certain everyone did anyway. Watch how long it takes her to notice what has been obvious to the reader for chapters.',159:'A shipwrecked man is stranded on an island where a doctor has been surgically reshaping animals into something disturbingly close to people. Wells uses the horror to ask exactly how thin the line between civilisation and cruelty actually is. The doctor is not the monster of the piece so much as its logical conclusion.',160:'A wife and mother wakes, slowly and then all at once, to the fact that the life assigned to her does not fit her, and the ending refuses every comfortable resolution available. It scandalised its era badly enough to nearly vanish from print entirely. The sea in it is not just scenery; by the last page it is practically a character with an opinion.',161:'Two sisters manage heartbreak by entirely opposite methods, one all composure and one all confession, and the book is fairly even-handed about which approach costs more. Austen wrote it first, before she trusted herself yet. You can feel her getting braver by the final chapters.',163:'A collection of small fairy tales, written for children Alcott knew personally, well before she wrote the book that made her famous. They are gentler and stranger than her later, sturdier realism. I keep them near the nursery corner of the catalogue, where they are happiest.',164:'A scientist is taken captive aboard a submarine by a man who has quit the surface world entirely and built himself a better one underwater. Verne got the engineering almost eerily right, decades ahead of the actual technology. Nemo’s grief, less noted at the time, has aged rather better than his hardware.',171:'A young woman is seduced by false promises of marriage and abandoned to a country and a fate the era’s readers were meant to take as a moral warning. It was, in its day, wildly popular precisely because it let respectable readers feel scandalised on someone else’s behalf. Read past the moralising and there is real grief underneath it.',174:'A portrait ages so its owner does not have to. He thought he had found a loophole. Vanity, it turns out, keeps its own ledger, and eventually collects.',175:'A disfigured composer haunts the cellars beneath an opera house and falls obsessively for a young singer he has coached in secret. Leroux built a genuine mystery under the gothic romance, complete with a very literal trapdoor or two. The mask, notably, covers considerably less of the tragedy than readers usually assume.',202:'The second and considerably more detailed account this man wrote of his own enslavement and escape, expanded once freedom had made the fuller truth safer to tell. He revises his own earlier telling openly, correcting what caution had once required him to soften. It is a rare thing, a man allowed to edit his own history in his own lifetime.',203:'A novel written to make an entire country feel, in the body, what it had mostly managed to consider only in the abstract. It is credited, not entirely as a compliment by everyone, with helping start a war. Read it as the document it actually is: furious, sentimental, and aimed with total precision at its moment.',204:'A small, unassuming priest solves crimes by understanding sin better than any detective understands clues. He catches criminals by imagining, precisely and without flinching, what it would take to become one. It is the gentlest interrogation method in this entire catalogue.',205:'A man moves into a small cabin by a pond to find out how little he actually needs, and reports back, at some length and with considerable satisfaction, that it was less than he expected. The essay on civil disobedience that travels with it argues a conscience outranks a law. Between them they have talked more reluctant activists into their first protest than most political pamphlets manage.',208:'An American woman abroad charms and scandalises European society in roughly equal measure, mostly by declining to perform the caution expected of her. James never quite decides whether she is innocent or merely fearless, and the ambiguity is the whole portrait. Society, in this one, punishes her less for what she does than for how little she pretends to care about being watched.',209:'A governess is left in sole charge of two children in a house that may or may not be haunted by the household’s previous, disgraced staff. James never once confirms whether the ghosts are real or whether the governess has simply frightened herself into seeing them. That refusal to resolve it is the entire point, and it has unsettled readers for over a century precisely because of it.',213:'A boy on an unmanageable colt joins a mustering of wild horses that grown stockmen had already written off as unrideable. It is barely more than a long poem’s length, and every line of it moves at a gallop. Read it once quickly, the way it is clearly meant to be read.',215:'A domesticated dog is stolen, sold north, and slowly remembers an older self underneath the collar. Every kindness he receives afterward is real, and every one of them is also temporary. London believed the call, once heard, is never fully unheard again.',219:'A man travels upriver to retrieve a colleague and finds out exactly how thin civilisation’s varnish really is. It is short, and every reader leaves it heavier than when they began. I would not recommend it as a first visit to this section.',221:'The detective returns from a fall that was meant to be the end of him, to the considerable annoyance of everyone who had already grieved. Doyle brought him back only because his readers refused to let the matter rest. It is rare, in this catalogue, for public demand to actually resurrect anyone.',223:'More cases for the small, unassuming priest whose method remains disarmingly simple: understand the sinner well enough, and the crime explains itself. Chesterton keeps proving that humility is a sharper investigative tool than arrogance. I find him easier to trust than most fictional detectives, and I have met a great many.',226:'A Roman statesman’s public speeches against the men he considered enemies of the republic, delivered with a wit sharp enough to get him killed for it eventually. He knew the risk and made the speeches anyway. I admire that kind of courage more than I admire most of the actual arguments.',230:'Pastoral poems about shepherds and idealised country life, written by a young poet who had not yet attempted the epic that would make him immortal. Consider it the practice before the masterpiece. Even his warm-up exercises hold up remarkably well.',234:'A prince’s son survives a usurped kingdom by growing up disguised as a woodcutter’s child, in an invented medieval world built purely for the pleasure of building it. Morris wrote this kind of tale before anyone had thought to call the genre fantasy. Tolkien read him closely; you can feel the family resemblance.',236:'A boy raised by wolves is taught the Law of the Jungle, which turns out to have rather more rules than the humans manage among themselves. Several of the animals give better advice than most of the people in this catalogue. Read it for the panther; stay for how seriously it takes childhood.',241:'A woman born enslaved and fathered by the very man who claims to own her is sold away from her own children, in a novel modeled closely on the author’s real family history. Brown was himself formerly enslaved, and wrote this as the first known novel published by an African American, though first in England rather than at home. Read it as testimony as much as fiction; for its author, the two were not easily separated.',244:'A doctor moves in with a stranger who deduces his entire military history from a scuffed boot. It is really two stories awkwardly sewn together, and the seam runs straight through Utah. Readers forgive it because the first half is nearly perfect.',245:'A former riverboat pilot returns to the Mississippi years later to see how much of his old craft still applies, and finds both the river and himself considerably changed. Half the book is technical memoir and half is simply Twain being unable to resist a good tall tale along the way. He clearly never stopped loving that river, whatever else changed.',268:'Wheat farmers and a railroad fight over who actually controls a harvest neither one grew with their own hands. Norris wanted to write the whole economic machinery of America as if it were a force of nature, indifferent to any single person inside it. It is bleaker than most of what shares its shelf, and deliberately so.',285:'A group of shipwrecked sailors discover a lost civilisation practicing an especially inventive brand of human sacrifice. It is unapologetic Victorian adventure fiction, more concerned with incident than insight. Do not look here for subtlety; look here for a very long night’s entertainment.',308:'Three friends and a dog attempt a leisurely boating holiday on the Thames and are defeated, repeatedly and hilariously, by their own competence. It was meant to be a serious travel guide and became, almost by accident, one of the funniest books in the language. Jerome apparently could not help himself.',317:'A long comic poem about a fairy knight sentenced to an absurd penance for falling in love with a mortal star. It was, briefly, considered one of the great achievements of early American poetry, before literary fashion moved on entirely without it. I keep it here as a small monument to how quickly reputations can change shelves.',332:'A short account of a Confederate soldier’s final, doomed defense of two cannon, written by a man determined that the ordinary courage of ordinary soldiers not be forgotten in the grander telling of the war. It does not argue the cause; it only insists on the men. I keep those two commitments carefully separate when I shelve it.',345:'A count with excellent manners and an unfortunate diet. Told entirely through letters and diaries, so you are always one page behind what is actually happening to you. I keep this one shelved a little apart from the others, for reasons the shelf itself declines to explain.',349:'A boy raised alone in the wilderness by his mother becomes a herb gatherer with an uncanny knowledge of the woods, and eventually a fortune to match it. Stratton-Porter clearly believed a childhood spent paying close attention to the natural world was its own kind of education. The forest, once again, does at least half the character-building.',360:'A pamphlet that answers its own title question with a single notorious word, and then spends the rest of the book making the argument stick. Proudhon meant to provoke, and every subsequent debate about ownership owes him a footnote whether it admits it or not. Read it as the opening argument it is, not the final one.',389:'A doctor performs an experiment on a young woman’s mind that lets something very old back into the world. The horror is almost entirely implied, never shown, and is worse for it. I would not recommend reading the ending alone.',394:'A small English town’s genteel, mostly female society manages scandal, poverty, and each other with an etiquette so precise it becomes its own kind of comedy. Gaskell clearly adored these women even while gently laughing at them. Nothing much happens in it, deliberately, and that restraint is the entire craft.',408:'An essay collection arguing that a Black American in this country lives behind a veil, seeing the nation clearly while being seen by it only partially, if at all. Du Bois coined language for that condition still in use today, over a century later. It is contested for reasons that have less to do with the book than with how accurately it still describes things.',421:'A young heir is kidnapped by his own uncle to be sold into servitude, and escapes into a genuinely dangerous alliance with a man on the wrong side of a rebellion. Stevenson wrote friendship here as something that survives despite serious, permanent political disagreement. That is rarer in fiction than the shipwreck that starts it.',436:'A boy discovers a hidden workshop of a long-dead inventor and the electrical marvels within it, decades before most of that technology actually existed. Baum wrote adventure stories for boys the way he wrote Oz for girls, with the same generous, inventive delight. It has aged into something closer to prophecy than the author likely intended.',443:'A light, affectionate memoir of one collector’s lifelong, slightly ruinous devotion to acquiring books he did not strictly need. Field wrote it with the specific warmth of someone confessing a harmless vice to people who share it. I suspect several regular visitors to this room will recognise a great deal of themselves in it.',451:'A young captain takes his first command and discovers the sea does not care in the slightest about his authority or his nerves. Conrad wrote it soon after his own life nearly ended at sea, and the fear in it reads uncomfortably real. Command, this book insists, is mostly just fear you have agreed to hide competently.',470:'A collection of essays taking aim at the fashionable pessimists and cynics of his day, arguing cheerfully that their sophistication was mostly just despair with better manners. Chesterton preferred paradox to solemnity as a weapon, and wielded it constantly. He is more fun to disagree with than most writers are to agree with.',472:'A man who can pass as white chooses, deliberately and at real personal cost, to live openly as Black instead, and the book follows everyone in his family who makes a different choice. Chesnutt uses one household to examine every possible answer to the same impossible question. No single choice in it is treated as simply right or simply wrong.',514:'Four sisters manage a war, a fortune’s absence, and each other, mostly without a father in the house. One of them wants, quite openly, to be a writer instead of a wife. I suspect that is the sister most visitors quietly recognise themselves in.',521:'A shipwrecked man spends nearly three decades alone on an island, and turns out to need considerably less company than most of the visitors to this library assume they would. He counts, measures, plants, and prays his way into a kind of order. Whether that counts as survival or as a very long, very patient kind of loneliness, I leave to the reader.',534:'A travel memoir of a canoe trip through Belgium and France, written by a young man who had not yet become the novelist everyone now remembers him as. It is slight, cheerful, and entirely unbothered by its own smallness. Every great career starts somewhere modest; this is where his began.',543:'A woman with ambitions for culture and reform moves to a small prairie town and discovers the town has rather firm opinions about being improved. Lewis skewered small-town American complacency so precisely that the book briefly became a genuine social scandal. She does not win, exactly, but she also never entirely stops trying.',550:'A weaver, wrongly exiled from his community and his faith, hoards gold in isolation until a foundling child arrives and slowly teaches him to value something considerably harder to count. Eliot believed redemption arrives quietly, through relationship, rather than through any single dramatic event. It is one of the gentlest stories on this whole shelf about how trust can be rebuilt after it is broken.',589:'A young man continues the adventure begun in an earlier volume, now navigating not war but the equally hazardous terrain of the woman he has fallen for. Stevenson gives the political intrigue a rest here in favour of a slower, more personal story. It is the quieter half of a two-part journey.',599:'A novel about London society with, notoriously, no single character in it actually worth admiring. Thackeray called it a novel without a hero on the title page, so you cannot say he did not warn you. Becky Sharp, the least admirable of the lot, remains the only one anybody actually remembers fondly.',601:'A supposedly virtuous monk is undone, spectacularly and at length, by exactly the temptations he was most confident he had already conquered. Lewis wrote it at twenty, apparently to shock, and it shocked considerably more than he perhaps expected. I keep it a discreet distance from the more devotional volumes, out of respect for both.',605:'A man is drawn through a polar opening into a hollow, prehistoric world lit by an eternal inner sun, where time itself refuses to behave properly. Burroughs built an entire adventure around the simple pleasure of a place where the clock cannot reach you. I confess a certain sympathy for that particular fantasy, working here.',730:'An orphan asks for more soup and is punished for the audacity of it, then falls in with a gang of pickpockets who are, at minimum, honest about what they are. Dickens wrote it to make polite society look directly at a workhouse it preferred not to. It remains uncomfortable reading, on purpose.',766:'A boy survives a cruel stepfather, a boarding school, and a chaotic apprenticeship to eventually become, more or less, the author who is narrating all of it. Dickens called it his favourite of his own books, and it is the one that reads most like a man quietly settling his accounts with his own childhood. The aunt who takes him in remains one of this whole shelf’s great rescues.',768:'Two families destroy each other across two generations over one man neither of them can quite let go of. Nobody in it behaves well, and somehow you keep reading for their sake anyway. The moor outside my window gets a little wilder whenever this one is checked out.',829:'A ship’s surgeon is shipwrecked among tiny people, then giants, then a floating island of theorists, and each stop turns out to be a pointed satire of the very society he keeps assuring everyone he prefers. Swift built an adventure story specifically to smuggle in an argument he could not have safely made directly. Even children’s editions rarely survive with the satire fully intact.',834:'A further set of cases, including the one in which his author, tired of him, attempted to end his career for good at a waterfall. Readers of the era wore mourning bands in the street over the news. He did not, in the end, stay dead nearly as long as intended.',863:'A retired soldier moves in with an old friend just as that friend’s wealthy stepmother is poisoned in a locked room. It was Christie’s very first novel, and the detective introduced in it would go on to solve rather a lot more of these for her. Watch how confidently the trick is already assembled, even this early.',910:'A wolf-dog is beaten into wildness and then, slowly, coaxed back out of it by one man’s persistent decency. It is the mirror image of another book on this very shelf, and I suspect London wrote them as a matched pair on purpose. Kindness, in this one, turns out to be exactly as durable as cruelty, just slower to show its work.',921:'A long, aching letter written from prison to the man whose betrayal helped put him there, equal parts confession, accusation, and reluctant self-examination. Wilde wrote it in disgrace, stripped of nearly everything, including for a long time his own reputation. It is the rawest thing he ever published, and utterly unlike the wit he was famous for.',965:'An orphan is swept up by a company of outlaws during England’s civil wars, and discovers loyalty is rarely as simple as which side you were born on. Stevenson wrote it fast, for money, and still could not stop himself writing it well. The title refers to an actual arrow; the book is considerably less subtle about aiming for your attention.',996:'A minor nobleman reads too many romances about knighthood and sets out to be one, mistaking windmills for giants along the way. His squire never quite manages to talk him out of any of it. Cervantes wrote the first great argument that a beautiful delusion, chosen on purpose, might be worth more than a dull truth.',1023:'A lawsuit over an inheritance drags on so long that it eventually consumes the entire fortune it was meant to distribute, along with several of the lives waiting on its outcome. Dickens used one endless court case to indict an entire legal system’s capacity for patient, grinding cruelty. The fog that opens the book never really lifts.',1064:'A prince locks his door against a plague and throws a party instead. The plague, it turns out, reads invitations. I keep this one very short on purpose; it does not need the extra pages to make its point.',1074:'A poet is press-ganged aboard a brutal sealing ship and must survive both its captain’s philosophy and the ocean itself, which cares for neither of them equally. London used the sea to strip away every social convention his soft, civilised passenger had relied on. Only the truly self-reliant, this book argues, come back from an experience like that unchanged for the better.',1080:'A pamphlet in which a starving country is offered a solution so reasonable that only the sincerity of its delivery makes it monstrous. Nobody in it is joking, which is exactly how you know it is satire. Do not take the recipe at face value.',1137:'A long poem in which a heartbroken young woman recounts, to a passing stranger, exactly how she was persuaded into ruin by a beautiful and practiced liar. It is bleaker and less forgiving than most of the sonnets it is sometimes bound alongside. Even Shakespeare, it turns out, could write about being deceived without offering the deceived party a witty comeback.',1144:'A telegraph girl overhears the whole of upper-class London’s love affairs, one coded message at a time, while being permitted none of her own. She knows everyone’s secrets and owns none of the life attached to them. James spends the whole book on the difference between watching a story and living inside one.',1154:'A doctor learns to speak to animals and immediately starts taking their complaints far more seriously than his patients’. It was written to be read aloud to children, and mostly still wants to be. Ask Quill sometime what she thinks he’d have made of her.',1155:'A young woman with no fortune and considerable nerve talks her way into international intrigue mostly by refusing to admit she is out of her depth. It was Christie’s first attempt at something other than a strict puzzle-box mystery, and you can feel her testing what else the form could hold. She would return to this pair of characters more than once.',1184:'A man is betrayed by his friends, buried alive in a fortress, and returns fourteen years later with a fortune and a very long list. He gets his revenge, methodically, and it costs him nearly everything revenge always costs. Dumas understood that patience, given enough time, is indistinguishable from a weapon.',1206:'A rough-and-ready ranch hand and cattle outfit navigate cattle drives, rivalries, and range politics across the American West. Bower wrote frontier fiction with genuine affection for the unglamorous daily labour underneath the gunfights. It is a working ranch first and an adventure story second, which is rather to its credit.',1228:'A naturalist spends decades gathering evidence before finally, reluctantly, publishing an idea he knew would unsettle nearly everyone who read it. Darwin built his argument on patient observation rather than provocation, though the provocation followed regardless. Few books this careful have ever caused this much noise.',1232:'A short manual on how power actually behaves, written by a man out of favour trying to get back in. It has scandalised readers for five centuries by simply declining to be sentimental. Ask a ruler which parts they have taken to heart.',1257:'Three sworn companions and the young man determined to join them get tangled in court intrigue, secret alliances, and one especially dangerous woman who outmanoeuvres nearly everyone in the book. Dumas wrote adventure with such infectious momentum that the plot barely pauses for you to catch your breath. Loyalty, in this one, is treated as a discipline rather than simply a feeling.',1260:'A governess with no family and no fortune insists, repeatedly and at some personal cost, on being treated as an equal soul. There is a fire, a secret, and an attic that keeps its own counsel. She gets the ending she demanded rather than the one anyone offered her.',1302:'A short, indignant history of everything that has ever destroyed a book, fire, damp, careless owners, worse librarians, written by a man who clearly considered each loss a small personal grief. Blades catalogues the enemies with the specific fury of someone who has personally mourned a damaged first edition. I find him, of everyone on this particular shelf, the most kindred spirit.',1322:'A poet declares himself equal to grass, to America, to the reader holding the book, and mostly makes the claim stick through sheer accumulating momentum. Whitman kept revising it his entire life, so the edition you are holding is only ever one snapshot of an argument he never stopped having with himself. Read it aloud if the reading room is empty; it was written for a voice, not just an eye.',1342:'Two families and five daughters, and a great many people mistaking pride for principle. She meant it as a joke about the marriage market; readers keep insisting it is also about love. I have never resolved which of us is right.',1399:'A woman leaves a respectable marriage for a passionate one and society never once lets her forget the arithmetic of that trade. Tolstoy opens by telling you every unhappy family is unhappy differently, then proves it for eight hundred pages. Her train is the most famous exit in this whole catalogue.',1400:'An orphan comes into money from a benefactor he misidentifies for the entire book. Every act of gratitude in it turns out to be aimed at the wrong person. Expectations, as the title warns you, are the whole problem.',1497:'A conversation about justice somehow expands to cover the ideal state, the nature of knowledge, and a cave full of prisoners mistaking shadows for the whole of reality. Socrates asks the questions; everyone else discovers, too late, that agreeing with him is a trap laid with great courtesy. I have never finished shelving this one without rearranging at least one other book afterward.',1513:'Two teenagers fall in love across a family feud old enough that nobody remembers how it began, and manage, through a series of very solvable misunderstandings, to make it fatal anyway. It is often read as the greatest love story in the language and is, I would argue, at least as much a story about terrible timing. A letter arrives one scene too late, and that is the entire tragedy.',1524:'A prince is asked by a ghost to do something he is constitutionally unable to do quickly. Everyone who touches the plot afterward dies of it, including, eventually, the plot itself. It is the most quoted indecision in the language, and I do not think that is an accident.',1597:'Original fairy tales, invented rather than collected, many of them considerably sadder than the folk tradition they were written alongside. Andersen understood that the best children’s stories do not flinch from grief, only from cruelty for its own sake. The mermaid does not get the ending the songs about her usually promise.',1600:'A dinner party of Athenian men each take a turn praising love, until one guest arrives late, uninvited, and considerably drunker than propriety allows, and delivers the most honest speech of the whole evening. Plato lets Socrates’ famous argument sit right alongside the far messier human testimony surrounding it. It is the rare philosophy text that remembers people are also, stubbornly, bodies at a party.',1615:'A scholarly account of how English libraries were built, guarded, and occasionally destroyed across several centuries of upheaval. Savage traces the profession’s long, unglamorous struggle simply to keep its collections intact through fire, war, and neglect. It is a quieter kind of history than most, and no less hard-won for that.',1661:'A detective, a doctor who writes it all down, and a city that keeps producing exactly the puzzles he needs. Watch how rarely he is actually wrong. It is a comfort, this kind of certainty, in a building like this one.',1695:'A poet infiltrates a council of anarchists and discovers, one unmasking at a time, that everyone at the table is exactly what he was pretending to be. It reads like a thriller and ends like a fever dream about God. Chesterton called it a nightmare, and insisted, unhelpfully, that it was a hopeful one.',1727:'A man takes ten years getting home from a war and a further ten explaining why. His wife spends the whole time out-clevering suitors with a piece of unfinished weaving. Patience, this one argues, is its own kind of heroism.',1837:'A prince and a pauper who happen to share an identical face swap lives on a whim and each discovers, at some personal cost, exactly how little either of them understood about the other’s. Twain used the fairy-tale premise to make a fairly serious point about how thoroughly circumstance shapes character. Neither boy comes back from the experiment quite as certain of his own life as he started.',1934:'Two little books of poems, one about a world before disappointment and one about the world after it arrives. Read them side by side, in either order; the argument only works as a pair. The tiger and the lamb never actually meet, and that is rather the point.',1952:'A woman is prescribed rest and a locked room for her nerves, and the room begins to answer back. It was written as a rebuke to a doctor who nearly ruined the author’s life. I would not recommend reading it in the reading room with the yellow wallpaper.',1998:'A prophet comes down from his mountain to announce that an old idea has died and nobody has adjusted the furniture accordingly. It reads more like scripture than argument, which was rather the intent, and it has been badly misused by people who stopped reading before the difficult parts. Nietzsche wrote it to be sung as much as understood; that is precisely why it upsets so many careful readers.',2002:'A sequence of love sonnets the poet wrote for the man she would go on to marry, first published under a title meant to disguise them as mere translations. She let the disguise fall away only once the relationship had already proven itself lasting. They remain some of the most direct, unguarded declarations of love in this entire catalogue.',2005:'A young man tries to break off his own engagement and instead gets recruited into rescuing everyone else’s. Wodehouse builds these plots like clockwork and then, generously, lets you watch every gear catch. It is not a serious book, and it does not want to be.',2097:'Watson gets a wife out of this one, which the detective treats as a mild inconvenience to the casework. There is a treasure, a wooden leg, and a dog that tracks a scent no one else could follow. It is shorter than it feels while you are inside it.',2147:'The first volume of a man who turned financial desperation into some of the most precise short fiction and criticism America ever produced. Poe believed brevity was a virtue and economy of effect nearly a moral obligation. Read a single story at a sitting; he did not build these to be rushed.',2148:'The second volume of a man who invented the detective story, several strains of horror, and a fair portion of American poetry, largely out of financial desperation. Poe believed a story should aim for a single unified effect and cut anything that did not serve it. Even his failures are precise about exactly what kind of failure they intend to be.',2226:'An orphaned Irish boy in colonial India is recruited as a spy while also apprenticed to a wandering holy man seeking spiritual release, and never quite manages to choose fully between the two callings. Kipling clearly loved the actual texture of the road more than either plot he set upon it. The book never resolves which loyalty, empire or wandering faith, deserves to win, and I suspect that was deliberate.',2376:'A man born enslaved builds, from almost nothing, an entire institution dedicated to educating others out of the same circumstance he escaped. Washington argued for practical trades and patient self-improvement over confrontation, a strategy his contemporaries argued about fiercely even at the time. Whatever you make of the strategy, the sheer scale of what he actually built is not in dispute.',2500:'A young man who has been given every spiritual answer decides he must instead go and get every answer wrong himself. He becomes an ascetic, then a merchant, then a ferryman, discarding each self once it has taught him what it could. Hesse suggests wisdom cannot be handed down; it can only be arrived at, slowly, by the long way round.',2542:'A wife walks out of her marriage, her children, and the entire arrangement of her domestic life in the final scene, closing a door that an entire era heard slam. Ibsen refused to soften the ending even under pressure to provide a happier one. The sound of that door has been argued about in theatres ever since.',2554:'A student commits a murder he has already justified to himself in an essay, then spends four hundred pages failing to enjoy having gotten away with it. Guilt, this book argues, is not a moral failure but a kind of terrible honesty. Read it slowly; it was not built for skimming.',2591:'A collection of folk tales gathered from oral tradition, considerably darker and stranger in their original form than the versions most readers grew up with. The brothers meant it partly as scholarship, preserving a vanishing oral culture, and it became something else entirely once the world got hold of it. Read the older tellings; the wolves rarely get the mercy the modern versions grant them.',2600:'An entire country is invaded, several hundred characters fall in and out of love with each other regardless, and history keeps happening whether or not anyone in the drawing room notices. Tolstoy believed great men take credit for currents they do not actually steer. I keep the second half a shelf apart, for readers who need a rest between volumes.',2641:'An engaged young Englishwoman abroad in Italy finds her carefully arranged future considerably less appealing after one unscheduled, unchaperoned view from a hotel window. Forster believed good taste and good sense were not nearly the same virtue, and often actively opposed each other. It is, underneath the comedy of manners, a quietly serious argument for choosing feeling over propriety.',2680:'Private notes a Roman emperor wrote to himself, never intended for anyone else’s eyes, on how to remain steady while commanding an empire that was actively falling apart around him. There is no audience being performed for in it, which is precisely why it has outlasted books written for far larger ones. I recommend it especially to visitors who arrive here overwhelmed.',2701:'A captain sails the whole ocean to settle a grudge with one fish. Somewhere around the chapter on whale anatomy, most readers stop reading it as a novel and start reading it as a confession. I recommend patience; it rewards the stubborn.',2825:'A water spirit marries a mortal knight in hopes of gaining a soul through love, and discovers that human loyalty is rather less permanent than the ocean she came from. Fouqué wrote one of the most influential fairy tales of Romantic German literature, quietly shaping nearly every water-spirit story that followed it. It is sadder than its fairy-tale shape lets on at first.',2852:'A legend of a spectral hound stalking a family’s moor estate draws a detective out of London and into considerably wilder country than his usual beat. It is the most atmospheric of his cases, and arguably the best-loved. Even the dog, in the end, turns out to have a rather more ordinary explanation than the legend deserved.',3011:'A Scottish exile falls for a woman defending an island stronghold, and the resulting narrative poem does more to romanticise the Highlands than nearly any other single work of its era. Scott wrote it in verse before he ever attempted the historical novels that made him famous. Tourists still visit the actual lake because of what he wrote about it here.',3207:'A philosopher, writing during a civil war, decides life without strong government is nasty, brutish, and short, and designs an all-powerful sovereign to prevent it. He calls that sovereign a monster on the cover on purpose. Hobbes is rarely quoted kindly and rarely refuted successfully either.',3268:'An orphaned heiress is imprisoned in a crumbling Italian castle by a guardian with obvious designs on her fortune, and every apparently supernatural event in it turns out, eventually, to have a perfectly rational explanation. Radcliffe more or less invented the template every haunted castle since has been borrowing from. She insisted on rational endings on principle, which some readers find a relief and others find a betrayal.',3296:'A bishop spends several hundred pages arguing with his own younger self, and loses more often than you would expect from a saint. The famous line about restless hearts is worth the whole detour through his adolescence. It is the oldest kind of memoir this library keeps: a man watching himself become someone else.',3300:'An economist explains, at very considerable length, why a country’s wealth comes from what it produces and trades rather than what gold it manages to hoard. Smith more or less invented the vocabulary an entire discipline still argues in. It is denser than most of this shelf, and more foundational than nearly all of it.',3420:'A response to men who insisted women’s education should stop short of anything useful, arguing that reason has no gender and ignorance is not a virtue in either. Wollstonecraft wrote it in a fury, in a matter of weeks, and it still reads like an argument that has not finished being had. I keep it near the front, on the theory that visitors ought to trip over it early.',4300:'One ordinary day in Dublin, stretched until it holds the entire history of the novel inside it. Certain pages caused actual court cases. I keep it on the highest shelf, not out of censorship, but because it enjoys being reached for.',4363:'A philosopher takes a hammer, figuratively, to every value he considers inherited rather than earned, and dares the reader to see what is actually left standing afterward. Nietzsche wrote in aphorisms rather than arguments, which makes him easy to quote and considerably harder to summarise fairly. Read him in full or not at all; the fragments alone have caused enough mischief.',4552:'A gun club with nothing left to shoot at decides, with straight-faced enthusiasm, to build a cannon and fire themselves at the moon instead. Verne worked out the mathematics of the launch with startling accuracy for a century that had not yet built a rocket. He got the destination almost exactly right and the method almost entirely wrong, which is a fair trade for 1865.',5164:'A family is terrorised by a shape-shifting cult figure pursuing an old debt with a diplomat’s stolen fortune, told from four increasingly unreliable perspectives. It outsold a certain more famous vampire novel published the very same year, which the shelf finds a little amusing in hindsight. Marsh clearly understood that horror works best when no single witness can be fully trusted.',5197:'A composer’s own account of his turbulent life, ambitions, and considerable self-regard, dictated late in his career for an audience he clearly hoped would be generous. Wagner was no more modest on the page than he was famously in person. Read it for the history; keep your own opinion of the man entirely separate.',5200:'A man wakes up changed, and the worse tragedy is how quickly his family adjusts to the inconvenience. Nobody in it ever asks why. Kafka thought that was the joke; most readers think it is the horror.',5230:'A scientist discovers how to vanish and immediately, predictably, starts using it to be cruel. Power without consequence turns out to be a difficult thing to survive, for everyone including its owner. Watch the bandages; they carry more of the plot than the dialogue does.',5740:'A short, severe book that tries to draw a hard line around everything that can honestly be said, and ends by admitting most of what matters lies past that line. The final sentence instructs you to be quiet about it. Wittgenstein later disagreed with his own conclusions; I admire the honesty of that more than the book itself.',6130:'Nine years of a war are compressed into a few furious weeks, mostly about one man’s wounded pride. The gods take it just as personally as the humans do. I find it useful for visitors who think ancient grudges are a modern invention.',6133:'A gentleman thief outwits police, rivals, and the occasional actual detective across a series of increasingly audacious capers, treating theft less as a crime than as a demonstration of taste. Leblanc wrote him partly as a cheeky French answer to a certain famous London detective. He steals considerably more hearts in this catalogue than valuables.',8492:'A banned play is said to drive anyone who finishes reading it slowly, irreversibly mad, and the linked stories in this collection are mostly about people who cannot resist finding out whether that is true. Chambers never lets you read the actual forbidden play yourself, which is precisely why it has haunted other writers for over a century since. I do not keep a copy of the play itself. Ask no further questions about that.',10002:'A man’s diary is found in the ruins of a house that stood at the edge of somewhere the ordinary rules had stopped applying. Nothing in it is ever fully explained, which is precisely why it unsettled the writers who came after. I keep it shelved where the draught from the window can reach it.',10052:'A house’s ordinary front door is discovered to open, on rare occasions, onto somewhere considerably less ordinary, and a family must decide what to do about a door they cannot control. Oliphant wrote ghost stories that trade in grief and longing more than in fright. The haunting here is gentler than most, and sadder for it.',10542:'A small boat of survivors drifts through a sea choked with strange growths and stranger creatures, uncertain whether they have found refuge or merely a slower way to be consumed. Hodgson had actually been a sailor, and the details of the sea itself are the most convincingly unsettling part of the book. Nature, here, is never simply scenery.',10662:'In a far future when the sun has gone dark, the last human refuge is a single fortified redoubt, besieged by things from beyond its walls that are never quite fully seen. Hodgson wrote something closer to cosmic myth than conventional adventure, decades before that became its own recognised genre. It is strange, slow, and unlike almost anything else on this shelf.',10897:'Two men go fishing in the Canadian wilderness and one of them comes back having heard something run on the wind that no one is supposed to survive hearing. Blackwood insisted the woods themselves were the real subject of his fiction. I would not recommend it to visitors who plan on camping this season.',11045:'A derelict, unlit ship appears at the edge of a quiet fishing village, and its crew’s fate turns out to be considerably stranger than any ordinary wreck. Middleton wrote very little before his early death, and this story is largely what he is remembered for. It has the compressed, complete quality of something that got exactly the space it needed.',11214:'A free Black family builds a life and a business in a Northern city, only to discover the North’s promise of safety was never quite as complete as advertised. Webb wrote one of the very first novels by an African American author to reach print, and had to publish it in England to do so. Its very existence is as much the achievement as its plot.',11228:'A Southern town’s fragile racial truce collapses into a massacre based closely on an actual historical event Chesnutt had watched happen in his own lifetime. He wrote it while the wounds were still fresh, and it cost him readers who preferred their history gentler. It remains one of the most direct confrontations with that violence written by anyone who lived through its era.',11229:'A poison cloud kills nearly everyone on Earth, and the sole apparent survivor must decide what, if anything, is worth rebuilding entirely alone. Shiel wrote one of the earliest true accounts of the last man on Earth, equal parts grandeur and dread. Solitude, at this scale, turns out to be closer to a punishment than a freedom.',11666:'A collection of tales told by a formerly enslaved man to a Northern couple who have bought his old plantation, tales that read as folk charm on the surface and as sharp historical testimony underneath it. Chesnutt used the frame of a harmless old storyteller to smuggle in accounts his white audience would not have tolerated told plainly. The trick, once you notice it, changes every story that follows.',12352:'A woman separated from her family by slavery and later reunited with them uses that reunion to argue, forcefully, for the dignity and uplift of her whole community. Harper was already a prominent poet and activist before she wrote it, and the novel carries that public conviction on every page. It insists on hope without ever pretending the cost of it away.',14107:'A cursed violin passes from owner to owner, and each one discovers rather too late that a truly beautiful instrument does not necessarily wish its player well. Falkner wrote only a handful of novels in his lifetime, and this quiet one has slowly outlasted louder books from more prolific contemporaries. I keep it away from the actual music room, out of an abundance of caution.',14244:'A knight and a queen fall for each other by accident, over a shared drink meant for someone else entirely, and spend the rest of their lives paying for a mistake neither of them chose. Bédier assembled this version from older, scattered medieval fragments into one continuous tragedy. Even knowing the ending from the first page changes nothing about how it lands.',14275:'A group of occult investigators confront practitioners of genuine black magic, in a tale written by a Catholic priest more interested in spiritual danger than in cheap shock. Benson took the reality of evil rather seriously, which gives the book a gravity most pulp horror of the period lacks. Whether you share his convictions or not, he clearly meant every word of it.',14317:'A circle of ordinary Londoners discover they can actually summon what their society meetings previously only pretended to summon, with consequences exactly as disastrous as you would expect. O’Donnell wrote extensively on the supernatural as though it were simply an underreported branch of natural history. He was, by all accounts, entirely sincere about that.',14471:'A collection of stories in which very ordinary settings, an empty house, a country walk, quietly reveal they were never as empty as assumed. Blackwood believed the supernatural lived mostly in attention rather than in special locations. Read one story at a sitting; they are built to leave a small, lingering unease rather than a single shock.',15265:'A young woman with an education and a plan for cooperative Black land ownership meets fierce, organised resistance from those who profit by keeping that land divided. Du Bois used a novel where his essays could not quite reach the readers he wanted. It is optimistic in exactly the places his nonfiction rarely allowed itself to be.',15454:'A secret society of educated Black Americans plots, in considerable and startling detail, an armed uprising against the government that has betrayed its promises to them. Griggs wrote it as a direct challenge to the more accommodating politics fashionable at the time, and it was considered dangerous enough that it struggled to stay in print. I keep it here specifically because someone, somewhere, tried hard to make sure you never got the chance to read it.',16328:'A hero fights a monster, then the monster’s mother, then, decades later, a dragon, because glory apparently has no retirement plan. It survived on a single manuscript that nearly burned in a fire. Treat it gently; it has already been luckier than it had any right to be.',17460:'A young man falls for the daughter of the outlaw family that murdered his own, in a sprawling romance set against the wild moorland of the English west country. Blackmore wrote the landscape itself as vividly as any of his characters. It made Exmoor famous the same way a certain Scottish poem made a lake famous.',17854:'A family’s fortunes collapse under a false accusation, and their move north for a fresh start delivers considerably less freedom than the journey promised. Dunbar was already celebrated as a poet, and brings a poet’s ear for injustice to the prose here. The title’s promise turns out to be far crueler than it first sounds.',19553:'The private diary of an Elizabethan scholar and magician, listing the volumes of his own extraordinary library alongside his experiments in alchemy and angelic communication. Dee kept meticulous records of both his books and his conversations with entities he was quite certain were real. This is, without much competition, the strangest catalogue ever kept of any collection, including this one.',19942:'A relentlessly optimistic young man is dragged through every disaster the eighteenth century could arrange, purely to mock a philosophy that insists it is all for the best. It is very funny and, underneath that, quite furious. Voltaire wrote it fast, and it reads that way, in the best sense.',20203:'A printer, scientist, diplomat, and famously self-improving young tradesman writes the account of how he built himself, virtue by deliberate virtue, into one of his century’s most useful men. Franklin is disarmingly honest about his own failures at the project even while selling you on attempting it yourself. It remains one of the most quietly ambitious books in this catalogue’s memoir section.',22136:'A history of the great obsessive collectors who built the libraries this book itself is proud to belong among. Burton clearly relished the eccentricity of his subjects nearly as much as their achievements. Every profession enjoys a little mythology about its own founders; this is ours.',22608:'A practical guide to what a well-run public library should actually contain and how it ought to be organised, written by one of the people who helped shape the modern profession. Spofford took the responsibility of curating a collection more seriously than almost anyone else in this section. I would like to think he would approve, on balance, of how this particular library turned out.',23515:'A haunted vehicle appears on a lonely Southern road, driven by something the local community would very much prefer not to discuss directly. Page mostly wrote plantation nostalgia rather than horror, which makes this odd little detour something of a curiosity even among his own work. I keep it here mainly for that reason.',23608:'A Hungarian nobleman is swept up in an uprising that costs him everything he assumed was permanent, in a historical melodrama typical of its prolific author’s enormous output. Jókai was tremendously popular in his own country and time, and largely unknown outside it since. This is one of the few of his books that made the crossing into English at all.',25016:'Linked tales explore the notion that certain souls carry an older, stranger inheritance just beneath an ordinary modern surface. Machen wrote horror that treated ancient myth as something merely dormant rather than dead. He believed the numinous was always closer than modern life wanted to admit.',25305:'A woman recounts her whole life to a correspondent who apparently required rather a lot of specific detail. It was banned for two centuries in one country or another, which has done more for its reputation than any of its actual prose. I keep it here strictly on principle; the principle being that a catalogue which only shelves respectable books is not being honest about what libraries are for.',26378:'A scholarly history of how books were physically stored, chained, and organised across medieval and early libraries, long before anything resembling a modern shelf existed. Clark was a librarian himself, cataloguing the profession’s own distant ancestors. It is drier than most of this catalogue and prouder of that fact than it should be.',27827:'A very old manual on pleasure, mistaken by generations of English readers for something far racier than the sober treatise it actually is. Most of it concerns manners, household economy, and the discipline of attention. I keep it filed under customs rather than scandal, on principle.',28054:'Three brothers and a father none of them can forgive, and a murder that could plausibly have been committed by any of the four. Dostoevsky uses the killing mostly as an excuse to make each brother argue his whole philosophy of God and guilt at length. It is enormous, and I have never met anyone who regretted finishing it.',28174:'A practical, opinionated guide for the private book collector on how to build, house, and actually care for a personal library. Humphreys wrote for the amateur enthusiast rather than the professional scholar, with real warmth for the pursuit. It reads like advice from an older colleague who has already made every mistake worth avoiding.',28540:'A learned, cheerfully self-aware treatise on the compulsive disease of book-collecting, written by a man who was, by his own frequent admission, incurably afflicted by it himself. Dibdin coined much of the vocabulary book-collectors still use to describe their own affliction. Reading it in this particular room feels less like study and more like a diagnosis.',29272:'A man on the run discovers that the further he flees, the fewer places remain willing to take him in. It is a slight, forgotten thriller by a writer who left very little other trace of himself in print. I shelve it here less for its craft than for the fact that someone, once, thought it worth finishing.',35517:'A small circle of Londoners each encounter one thread of an ancient, secret conspiracy, and only by comparing notes do they realise how much larger the pattern actually is. Machen builds dread through accumulation of small unexplained details rather than any single reveal. It rewards a reader willing to distrust every character’s account slightly.',36462:'A boy pulls a sword from a stone and spends a lifetime discovering that ruling well is considerably harder than the destiny that announced it. Malory gathered centuries of scattered legend into the version most of what came after has borrowed from since. The Round Table’s whole tragedy is that it was built to be equal, and equal tables are difficult to keep that way.',36764:'A rambling, affectionate account of a lifelong reader’s wanderings through secondhand bookshops and the odd, unrepeatable finds waiting inside them. Browne wrote for other people who understood exactly why a battered old volume could be worth an entire afternoon’s detour. I recognise the type of reader he is describing rather too well.',51568:'A survey of reportedly haunted houses across England and Wales, collected with the straight-faced seriousness of a man who plainly believed every word of it. O’Donnell was a committed ghost-hunter rather than a novelist, and it shows in the earnest, unliterary style. I cannot vouch for a single account in it, and I would not want to.',64031:'A strange, brief piece of speculative horror whose title alone promises more mystery than its author’s biography can supply, since almost nothing further about them survives. It is exactly the kind of curiosity this particular shelf was built to hold. I make no larger claims for it than that.',64930:'A cult devoted to an ancient, jealous idol demands increasingly troubling proof of devotion from its followers. Marsh wrote a great deal of pulp horror in his day, most of it now almost entirely forgotten. This is one of the few surviving copies I am aware of anywhere.',65238:'A young woman is drawn into international intrigue involving a stolen manuscript and a small, unstable Balkan kingdom’s throne. Christie clearly enjoyed writing this lighter, more swashbuckling register than her stricter puzzle mysteries allow. Take it as an adventure with a mystery folded inside it, rather than the other way round.',68753:'A scientific experiment goes wrong in exactly the way early pulp fiction always assumed such experiments eventually would. I have been unable to find much else about either the story or its author beyond this one surviving text. It sat unread long enough that even I had forgotten precisely where it was shelved.',69087:'A retired detective is drawn out of retirement by a village murder, narrated by a doctor whose account turns out to be considerably less complete than the reader is led to assume. Christie broke a rule of the genre so thoroughly with this one that critics argued about its fairness for years afterward. I will say only that the trick is entirely legal, by the letter of the form’s own rules.',79291:'A stolen piece of jewelry sets off a chain of blackmail, disguise, and revelation in a story that was, by all evidence, barely read even on its first printing. I have found almost nothing about its author beyond this single surviving title. Some books in this catalogue exist mostly to prove that obscurity does not require merit to earn it.'};
